  <revision rev="9" vrev="1">
    <srcmd5>651cc444c8ac2a2e9d3b174836469f8f</srcmd5>
    <version>1.13.0</version>
    <time>1793121528</time>
    <user>dimstar_suse</user>
    <comment>- update to 1.13.0:
  * Support the RFC 9535 logical operators &amp;&amp; and || in filters.
    (#27) For example, parse(&quot;$[?@.a == 1 || @.b == 2 &amp;&amp; @.c ==
    3]&quot;). Note that filters are currently only supported in the
    jsonpath_ng.ext module. &amp;&amp; binds more tightly than ||, and
    parentheses group expressions. The operators are valid only
    inside a filter.
  * Fix Slice.filter() taking quadratic time to remove array
    elements.
  * Fix Slice.filter() removing the wrong elements from an array.
  * Return [] when a slice's step size is 0. For example,
    parse(&quot;$[::0]&quot;).find([1,2,3]) now returns []. This aligns
    with RFC 9535, section 2.3.4.2.2.
  * Fix != filter comparisons with literals. This includes fixing
    comparisons between string and integer values, as well as
    fixing comparisons when the target path doesn't exist. For
    example, parse(&quot;$[?@.a != 1]&quot;).find([{&quot;b&quot;: 1}]) finds {&quot;b&quot;:
    1}. Previously, {&quot;b&quot;: 1} was excluded because it didn't have
    an &quot;a&quot; field. For example, parse(&quot;$[?@.a != 1]&quot;).find([{&quot;a&quot;:
    &quot;x&quot;}]) finds {&quot;a&quot;: &quot;x&quot;}. Previously, {&quot;a&quot;: &quot;x&quot;} was excluded.
    This change aligns with RFC 9535, section 2.3.5.2.2.
  * Fix a TypeError that occurs when a filter orders values of
    different types. For example, parse(&quot;$[?@.a &lt;
    1]&quot;).find([{&quot;a&quot;: None}]) now finds nothing instead of raising
    a TypeError.
  * Stop speculatively coercing strings to integers in filter
    comparisons. For example, parse(&quot;$[?@.a == 1]&quot;).find([{&quot;a&quot;: &quot;
    +0_1&quot;}]) now finds nothing. Previously, the string &quot; +0_1&quot;
    was converted to an integer before comparing and would
    incorrectly match. Note that this also means that simpler</comment>
    <requestid>1392632</requestid>
  </revision>
